WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — rce-compile (180s) Narrator Lem

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.867
First, real compilers break any single descriptive law, so this study defines a seven-part yardstick instead.

2
00:00:07.427 --> 00:00:11.685
Second, maximum compiler optimization burns upfront energy that

3
00:00:11.685 --> 00:00:15.132
never pays back on thirty percent of test programs.

4
00:00:15.692 --> 00:00:21.067
Third, fingerprinting code by variable position reuses builds with zero mix-ups,

5
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:24.090
cutting Rust compile energy four hundredfold.

6
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:31.197
An assistant system proposed a sweeping Law of Compilation claiming every compiler preserves meaning

7
00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:38.988
across canonical forms toward physical Landauer limits. Checked against twelve real compiler families—spanning C, Rust,

8
00:00:38.988 --> 00:00:46.909
Fortran, Haskell, verified CompCert, and tensor compilers—that universal description flatly failed on every major clause.

9
00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:52.793
Claiming every building already has fire sprinklers is false; writing a clear fire code

10
00:00:52.793 --> 00:00:59.341
lets inspectors measure every building honestly. Rewriting three clauses turns the refuted description into

11
00:00:59.341 --> 00:01:05.521
a seven-part normal form with four explicit equivalence levels and computable size and energy bounds.

12
00:01:06.081 --> 00:01:13.766
We compiled and executed twenty deterministic integer and fixed-point kernels across fifteen toolchains, verifying all three hundred

13
00:01:13.766 --> 00:01:20.461
output checksums. Pre-compiling the Reality Code Engine runtime once into a shared library cut emitted Rust compile

14
00:01:20.461 --> 00:01:28.496
energy twenty-point-one times, dropping its payback horizon against JavaScript from thirty-one thousand runs to one thousand seventy-nine.

15
00:01:29.056 --> 00:01:37.468
Lowering from Python to optimized C pays back after a median of twenty-four hundred ninety runs, whereas jumping from unoptimized builds to

16
00:01:37.468 --> 00:01:46.788
level-three optimization demands fifty-three thousand to one hundred two thousand runs—and stepping from level two to level three never recovers its extra

17
00:01:46.788 --> 00:01:55.806
compile energy on up to thirty percent of kernels. Meanwhile static code size predicts runtime energy poorly compared with timed execution profiling.

18
00:01:56.366 --> 00:02:04.597
Our three-dimensional model traces source lowering through explicit equivalence levels into a name-free positional fingerprint. Rather

19
00:02:04.597 --> 00:02:11.969
than keying build caches on developer variable names—or blindly stripping names—De Bruijn indexing numbers each variable

20
00:02:11.969 --> 00:02:19.033
by how many binding scopes away it was declared, preserving exact data-flow wiring while ignoring harmless renames.

21
00:02:19.593 --> 00:02:27.760
Scaled across one hundred eighty-five Reality Code Engine blueprints, sixty C, Rust, and Python kernels, and five hundred test-verified

22
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:37.499
semantic mutants, naive name-stripping suffered one hundred fifty collisions—failing on every single swapped-variable pair. Positional De Bruijn hashing achieved

23
00:02:37.499 --> 00:02:45.060
zero false matches while cutting warm C rebuild energy one hundred sixty-eight times and Rust four hundred forty-eight times.

24
00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:52.824
Three honest boundaries remain: byte-for-byte compiler output comparisons across three machines left unexplained binary

25
00:02:52.824 --> 00:02:59.000
differences even after path remapping, all energy figures come from on-chip Linux counters rather than

26
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:05.114
